    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "order": 49,
      "bbox": [
        453,
        194,
        467,
        458
      ],
      "margin_number": null,
      "speaker": null,
      "text": "[See note on the sophists on p. 57]",
      "lang": "en",
      "para_no": 1,
      "marker": null,
      "table": null,
      "exercise": null,
      "caption": null,
      "uncertain": []
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "order": 50,
      "bbox": [
        468,
        194,
        621,
        876
      ],
      "margin_number": null,
      "speaker": null,
      "text": "The sophists both helped to create a demand for education, and also came when\nthere was an unfulfilled need for it. They taught a vast variety of subjects—from\nastronomy and law through to mathematics and rhetoric. It is in large measure due\nto the sophists that subjects such as grammar, logic, ethics, politics, physics and\nmetaphysics first emerged as separate entities. The sophists were at the head of a\nmovement to make man, not the physical world, the centre of intellectual debate.\nIf their main preoccupation was to describe how man could be most successful\nin life, rather than with questions of right and wrong of the sort that Socrates and\nPlato insisted upon, this does not undermine their intellectual importance.",
      "lang": "en",
      "para_no": 2,
      "marker": null,
      "table": null,
      "exercise": null,
      "caption": null,
      "uncertain": []
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "order": 51,
      "bbox": [
