      "speaker": null,
      "text": "Much work was going on in other fields at this time too. If our sources can\nbe trusted, technical manuals were written by Sophocles on tragedy, by Iktinos\non the Parthenon, by Polykleitos on the symmetry of the human body, and\nby Hippodamos (who designed the layout of the Peiraeus) on town planning\nand social engineering. Rudimentary experimental work in sciences may also\nhave been going on, if we wish so to interpret the evidence of Aristophanes'\nClouds. When the rustic Strepsiades is introduced into Socrates' private school\n(φροντιστήριον or ‘think tank’), he finds all sorts of extraordinary devices\ncluttering up the place … These cosmic models (celestial globes? star maps?\ncompasses? maps?) are an important feature of the play, where the association\nbetween the new thought and its various trappings is constantly being made. It\nsuggests that the use of models and apparatus, generally seen as a later, post-\nAristotelian device, was understood well enough in fifth-century Athens to be\nmade the subject of comic humour. (World of Athens, 8.23–4)",
